 # LOD Selection and Culling
-
-
 ## Overview
 LOD Selection and Culling determines which resolution level of a multiresolution volume hierarchy to render for a given view or spatial region, balancing visual fidelity against memory and performance constraints [G]. It solves the problem of rendering large volumetric datasets that exceed hardware budgets by dynamically discarding invisible subregions (culling) and selecting coarse approximations for distant or less important areas (LOD selection) [G]. It differs from sibling methods like [Mesh Simplification](1032-mesh-simplification.md) by operating on volumetric data structures (bricks, octrees, splats) rather than polygonal meshes, and from [Wavelet-based Volume Rendering](1052-wavelet-based-volume-rendering.md) by focusing on the selection logic and data management rather than the specific transform decomposition itself [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family share the core mechanism of generating a hierarchy of data representations at different spatial resolutions [G]. This hierarchy is typically constructed using one of two approaches:
-1. **Spatial Hierarchies:** Structures like Octrees or Bricks that partition the volume domain into sub-regions of varying sizes [G].
-2. **Frequency-Domain Transforms:** Methods like Wavelets that decompose data into coefficients representing different spatial frequencies [G].
-
-Common mathematical and structural operations include:
-* **Scaling Factors:** Defining a scale factor $s$ such that the new resolution $N_{\text{new}}$ relates to the previous resolution $N_{\text{prev}}$ by $N_{\text{new}} = s \times N_{\text{prev}}$ or $N_{\text{new}} / N_{\text{prev}} = s$ [G].
-* **Interpolation and Filtering:** Using interpolation kernels (e.g., trilinear) or filtering to estimate values at new grid points or to generate coarser levels from finer ones [G].
-* **Selection and Culling:** Dynamically choosing which resolution level to render based on spatial hierarchy (distant or less important regions) and hardware constraints [G].
+All methods in this family share the core principles of multiresolution hierarchy generation and selection, as detailed in [Multiresolution and level of detail](7-multiresolution-and-level-of-detail.md).
 
